>2) The above point regardless, if _only_ the magical component of a
>weapon does damage to said enemy, shouldn't the physical component
>(i.e, the strength of the wielder, the sharpness of the blade) be
>subtracted from the damage calculation?

My impression is that the idea is that the creature has some manner of
protective energy field which is disrupted by sufficiently powerful
enchantments, or in some cases by weapons made of suitable materials.

(Runequest, on the other hand, has precisely the mechanic you describe;
if you whack a Runequest werewolf with a Truesworded great sword, you
only get the Truesword damage, not the base damage or your character's
melee damage bonus.)
